Decisions

Flags:
      --threshold NUMBER        Turn a yes or no probability into a boolean decision.
      --assert EXPR             Exit 1 when an answer fails this condition.
  -q, --quiet                   Suppress output. Without --assert, judge one answer.
      --min-confidence NUMBER   The confidence a pick or rate answer must reach. Needs
                                --fallback. Confidence is about 1 when p sits on one
                                answer.
      --fallback VALUE          Use this as answer.decision on low confidence or error.
                                JSON keeps the model answer in answer.value when
                                available.
      --abstain-if EXPR         Turn a no into an unsure when this condition holds.
                                Repeatable, combined with and. Needs --assert and
                                replaces the -f file's abstain_if.

How they work together:
  --threshold adds a boolean decision to a yes or no probability. A false decision alone
  does not change the exit code. Without --assert, quiet mode judges one question on one
  record. Yes or no uses the threshold, defaulting to 0.5. Pick and rate need both
  --min-confidence and --fallback. Quiet mode refuses streams.

  --min-confidence applies only to pick and rate, needs --fallback and takes a value
  between 0 and 1. Confidence is about 1 when p sits on one answer. Yes or no fallback
  values must be boolean spellings. Pick and rate can fall back to a review destination
  outside their options. JSON retains the model answer in answer.value when available,
  with the policy result in answer.decision. Low confidence has fallback reason
  low_confidence and an error has reason error. A fallback does not turn an error into
  an answer.

  Each record ends in one verdict: answered with no gate, pass, fail, unsure or error.
  --assert is a boolean expression over the record. Repeated assertions are combined
  with and and replace the file's assert. Acceptance is checked first. Only when it
  fails is --abstain-if checked. Repeated abstain expressions combine with and, need
  --assert and replace the file's abstain_if. A false assertion is a fail and exits 1.
  When --abstain-if holds instead, the record is unsure and exits 7. Quiet beside
  --assert only suppresses output.

  An --assert path is a question id, a dot and a field, as in urgent.value. Compare with
  ==, !=, <, <=, > and >=, test a string with in ["a", "b"], and combine with and, or,
  not and parentheses. Strings take double quotes. An id written bare holds only
  letters, digits and _, starts with a letter or _, and is not and, or, not, in, true or
  false. Write any other id in brackets and double quotes, as in ["needs review"].value,
  and an option the same way after p, as in team.p["on call"]. min, max, sum and avg
  take numbers and nest, as in max(a.value, b.value) < 0.2. There are no arithmetic
  operators, so weight answers with jq. A box has no value, so gate it on its edges or
  its confidence, as in mug.confidence >= 0.6.

  Streams print every fail and unsure too. --stop-on=fail prints the first fail before
  stopping, and --stop-on=unsure the first unsure. Both need --assert, and unsure also
  needs --abstain-if. Earlier errors still affect the aggregate exit code. See Asking
  for question setting scope. Use onesie help calibrate to choose cuts from labelled
  evidence. The export example uses an illustrative cut.

  Inspect a policy without asking the model:
    onesie --ask 'urgent=is this urgent' --threshold 0.5 --print-questions

